Transport trait for dspi-core: let the core run over links other than libusb
(DSPi Link first), with the four behaviours that differ over such a link.

Why: the core is hard-wired to rusb (`UsbConnection` wraps a
`DeviceHandle`, the notify listener and the analyser worker read the handle
directly). The Android app's DSPi Link client (dspi-ffi `link.rs` over the
`dspi-link` crate) must drive the same core over a WebSocket to a hub
(DSPi-Link spec 1.2, WeebLabs/DSPi-Link 86f10e3). Investigation:
dspi-mobile/docs/DSPI-LINK-INVESTIGATION.md section 3.1.

Shape (small on purpose; the USB path is unchanged):
- `usb::Transport` (Send + Sync): `write_control`, `read_control`,
  `read_notification` (verbatim packet + optional Link `origin`),
  `clear_notification_halt`, `session_id`, `caps` (`TransportCaps`:
  `chunked_bulk`, `max_payload`) and `lock`/`unlock` (default: no locks).
  Errors stay `rusb::Error`, so every existing match keeps working; a
  non-USB transport maps a refused request to `Pipe`, a timeout to
  `Timeout`, a lost link to `NoDevice`. (A future shared core would give the
  trait its own error enum; that is a rename, not a redesign.)
- `usb::UsbTransport`: the libusb implementation (the old code, moved).
  `UsbConnection::new(handle)` keeps its signature and wraps it, so
  `select_device` and 0001's `select_device_handle` are untouched apart from
  `shared_handle()` -> `transport()`.
- `DeviceManager::select_transport(transport, serial, vid)`: connect over
  an embedder's transport with the same bookkeeping as `select_device`.

The four behaviours (each a no-op over USB):
1. Echo suppression (notify.rs): a queued packet carries the transport's
   attribution. USB (origin None): every HOST_SET is ours, as upstream.
   Link: only origin == our session id is ours; origin 0 never is (Link
   spec 8.3: "must not suppress by source alone"). Without this a Link
   client never sees EQ or volume changes made by another client.
2. Bulk (commands.rs): `read_bulk` / `apply_all_params` use single-shot
   0xA0 / 0xA1 when `caps().chunked_bulk` is false (Link spec 8.2; a
   bridge hub refuses 0xA2/0xA3). 0xA0 is read with wLength 6136 and
   wIndex 2, as the macOS Console reads it.
3. Timeouts: the trait's calls carry the caller's USB budget (1 s, the
   analyser's 500 ms); a slower link applies its own (dspi-ffi's Link
   transport: 10 s, 15 s for bulk, the macOS LinkClient values).
4. Analyser fallback (rta.rs): the worker reads through the transport, so
   a Link `ERROR` for RTA bands-all (0x0F, USB-only; a bridge answers
   ERROR) mapped to `Pipe` takes the existing per-channel 0x0B fallback.

Tests: usb.rs (single-shot vs chunked bulk through a mock transport),
notify.rs (Link attribution rules).

Upstream form: identical; can be offered to WeebLabs/DSPi-Console-Linux as
is (the C ABI is untouched). Applies after 0001 (it edits the line 0001
adds in device.rs). Numbered 0004 so the unmerged 0003 (wip/wire-compat,
which also touches `fetch_all_params` and `apply_all_params` in
commands.rs) keeps its name; rebasing one onto the other is a one-line
context fix in commands.rs.

